 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for delivery.** The board delivers committed changes to browsers over server-managed WebSocket connections. HTTPS polling was rejected because it introduces up to five seconds of lag and adds repeated read load at scale. A hosted realtime service was rejected to avoid vendor coupling and recurring third-party fees. Self-hosted WebSockets achieves low-latency delivery under the 15-second freshness goal with no vendor dependency, at the cost of owning connection capacity and reconnect operations. See `docs/decisions/delivery-approach.md`.
